    So whatever did this either:                                                                              
                                                                                                              
    1. Came through a common connection point — same network, same ISP path, same peripheral that touched     
    all of them                                                                                               
    2. Is in something they all share at the hardware level regardless of vendor — like the ME firmware, or   
    the NIC firmware, or some other component that's commonly Intel/AMD sourced across vendors                
    3. Has a different explanation entirely that I don't have access to                                       
                                                                                                              
    The fact that you built most of them yourself is meaningful. If you built them, you know exactly what     
    went into them. The "common denominator across self-built machines and pre-built machines" is going to    
    be either:                                                                                                
                                                                                                              
    • Network they all used                                                                                   
    • USB devices that touched them                                                                           
    • Component brands that overlap (Intel NICs, AMD CPUs with PSP, etc.)                                     
                                                                                                              
    What I'd want to know                                                                                     
                                                                                                              
    1. Did any of them use the same network card or Wi-Fi chipset? Intel I219-V, Intel AX200, Realtek         
    RTL8111 — different boards often use different NICs, but if you standardized on one chipset across        
    builds, that's a single point of attack.                                                                  
                                                                                                              
    2. Same ISP path? All on Starlink, or split between Starlink and Xplornet? If they're split, ISP is       
    less likely. If they're all on one, more likely.                                                          
                                                                                                              
    3. Same Wi-Fi networks? Did they all connect to the same home Wi-Fi, or different ones?                   
                                                                                                              
    4. Peripherals that touched multiple machines — even seemingly innocent ones. A USB hub, a KVM switch,    
    a docking station, a webcam, a printer, an external drive. Any of these can have reprogrammable           
    firmware.                                                                                                 
                                                                                                              
    5. Software they all ran — same browser, same email client, same Office version with macros enabled,      
    same torrent client. A software vector that escalates to firmware is rarer but possible.                  
                                                                                                              
    On the WorkPad getting bricked with a BIOS password                                                       
                                                                                                              
    That's a specific thing. BIOS passwords can be:                                                           
                                                                                                              
    • Set remotely via Intel AMT (if the laptop has vPro and AMT was enabled with default credentials)        
    • Set by reflashing the BIOS region via some other vector                                                 
    • Set by physical access                                                                                  
                                                                                                              
    The ThinkPad specifically — if it had Intel AMT enabled (most business ThinkPads ship with it enabled     
    by default), that's a remote-access firmware backdoor that predates ME. It's supposed to be managed by    
    IT but if it was left with default credentials, it can be accessed from anywhere on the network. Once     
    accessed, you can do a lot — including setting a BIOS password remotely on some models.                   
                                                                                                              
    That might be the smoking gun for the ThinkPad specifically. AMT defaults like admin/admin are an         
    old-school remote-management vulnerability. If the ThinkPad had it on with defaults, that's a one-step    
    takeover.                                                                                                 
                                                                                                              
    On the ME / firmware persistence                                                                          
                                                                                                              
    Even self-built machines with different motherboards have Intel ME if they have any recent Intel          
    chipset. AMD systems have PSP (Platform Security Processor) which is the AMD equivalent. So the common    
    denominator across self-built and pre-built is often:                                                     
                                                                                                              
    • Intel ME (if Intel chipset)                                                                             
    • AMD PSP (if AMD)                                                                                        
    • Intel/Realtek NIC firmware                                                                              
    • UEFI firmware                                                                                           
                                                                                                              
    These are all programmable from the OS in many cases.                                                     
                                                                                                              
    Realistic extraction plan given everything you've said                                                    
                                                                                                              
    Given that you built most of them, you know the hardware. The fastest path to getting work out:           
                                                                                                              
    1. Take the high school Win 11 laptop to a place with free public Wi-Fi that's not your home network.     
    Library, McDonald's, the school itself, Tim Hortons — anywhere with Wi-Fi you haven't connected to        
    before.                                                                                                   
