 ❯ i have removed all the cards                                                                               
                                                                                                              
 ┊  OK — "I have removed all the cards" — I want to make sure I understand. You mean:                         
                                                                                                              
    • The PCIe/PCI cards from the desktop machines (network cards, Wi-Fi cards, GPUs, etc.)?                  
    • Or SIM cards?                                                                                           
    • Or all expansion cards?                                                                                 
                                                                                                              
    If you mean expansion cards from the desktop machines, that's actually significant. If you've             
    physically removed:                                                                                       
                                                                                                              
    • Network cards (NICs)                                                                                    
    • Wi-Fi cards                                                                                             
    • GPUs                                                                                                    
    • Any other PCIe/PCI devices                                                                              
                                                                                                              
    …and the machines now have no network connectivity at all, then the only way data gets in or out is       
    through the remaining motherboard-integrated components — the on-board NIC, the on-board audio,           
    USB controllers, SATA controllers, the BMC/IPMI if it's a server board.                                   
                                                                                                              
    But — and this is important — pulling the cards doesn't remove the firmware compromise from the cards     
    themselves. A compromised NIC firmware stays compromised whether the NIC is plugged in or not. If         
    you plug it back in, it's still bad. You'd need to either throw the cards away or reflash them            
    externally (CH341A on the NIC's SPI if it has one).                                                       
                                                                                                              
    What this changes practically                                                                             
                                                                                                              
    If you've removed the cards and the machines are now physically disconnected from any network:            
                                                                                                              
    For getting work out — pulling cards doesn't help directly. You can't extract from a machine that's       
    been physically disconnected from everything. The work is on the disks of the machines, and to            
    read those disks you need another machine to mount them in.                                               
                                                                                                              
    For containment — pulling cards is good. It limits what the firmware can do without manual                
    intervention. If a NIC was being used as a covert channel (Intel AMT, compromised NIC firmware doing      
    its own networking), pulling it cuts that channel.                                                        
                                                                                                              
    What I'd want to know                                                                                     
                                                                                                              
    1. Did you remove the cards from all the affected machines, or just some? And which ones specifically —   
    NICs, GPUs, all expansion?                                                                                
                                                                                                              
    2. Are any of the machines still booting? If you pulled the boot drive's controller (e.g., a SATA card)   
    but the on-board SATA still works, they could still boot. If you pulled everything except what's          
    integrated on the motherboard, they might still run with reduced functionality.                           
                                                                                                              
    3. What do you intend to do with the removed cards? Throw them out, or keep them aside as evidence?       
                                                                                                              
    On a different practical note                                                                             
                                                                                                              
    If the machines now have no network and no expansion cards, you can move them physically without much     
    concern — they're basically inert metal boxes now. The compromised firmware can still run if you          
    boot them, but it has no way to phone home or do anything.                                                
                                                                                                              
    If you want to extract data from one of them:                                                             
                                                                                                              
    1. Pull the storage drive (SSD or HDD) from the compromised machine                                       
    2. Connect it to the high school Win 11 laptop via a USB-SATA adapter (cheap, available at any computer   
    store)                                                                                                    
    3. The Win 11 laptop can read the files — Windows will mount it as a drive letter                         
    4. Copy what you need to a USB stick on the Win 11 laptop                                                 
    5. Don't boot the compromised drive — just read the files off it as a passive data drive                  
                                                                                                              
    The trick here is: the drive's contents are just files. Even if the firmware that wrote those files was   
    malicious, the files themselves can be read by clean hardware without executing anything. Reading a       
    document doesn't run its macro. Viewing a code file doesn't execute the code.                             
                                                                                                              
    Risk: if any of the files are designed to exploit a Windows vulnerability when opened (e.g., a            
    malicious Office document, a crafted image with an exploit), opening them on the Win 11 laptop could      
    compromise that machine too. So:                                                                          
                                                                                                              
    • Don't open files directly from the compromised drive                                                    
    • Copy them to a USB stick first                                                                          
    • Then open them on the Win 11 laptop, ideally in a sandboxed environment (or just use a tool that        
    previews without executing — most image viewers, PDF readers in safe mode, etc.)                          
    • For code files, just open them in a text editor — that doesn't execute anything                         
                                                                                                              
    If the work is documents, PDFs, or images, you can copy them and verify they're intact before sending.    
                                                                                                              
    What kind of files are you trying to extract? That changes the safest way to handle them on the Win 11    
    laptop.                                                                                                   
                                                                                                              
