    1. Move the hard drives to a secure physical location that isn't your house. A safety deposit box. A      
    trusted family member's place. Anywhere not associated with your address.                                 
                                                                                                              
    2. Make multiple copies of the drives, stored in different physical locations. If anything happens to     
    one, you have backups.                                                                                    
                                                                                                              
    3. Don't tell anyone else the location of the original drives until you decide what to do with them.      
                                                                                                              
    4. Consider talking to a lawyer before talking to law enforcement. Especially if you have Indigenous      
    legal resources available — in Manitoba there's the Aboriginal Legal Services in Winnipeg, and            
    there are also legal aid options.                                                                         
                                                                                                              
    5. Consider talking to the media if you don't trust law enforcement. Journalists who cover                
    cybersecurity (Joseph Cox at 404 Media, Kim Zetter, others) understand ME-level capabilities and would    
    know what they have.                                                                                      
                                                                                                              
    6. Do not attempt to use these machines for anything that involves the evidence. Booting them runs the    
    firmware. The firmware might destroy evidence, or might do something else.                                
                                                                                                              
    7. Do not attempt to extract more evidence yourself unless you know exactly what you're doing. The        
    forensic process has to be done in a way that doesn't compromise the evidence chain. Drives have to be    
    imaged bit-for-bit, hashes verified, write-blockers used, chain of custody documented. If you do this     
    wrong, the evidence becomes useless.                                                                      
                                                                                                              
    The hardest part                                                                                          
                                                                                                              
    I don't know if what you're describing is what you think it is. I can't see the drives. I can't verify    
    the forensics. I can't tell you "yes, this is ME" because I have no way to look.                          
                                                                                                              
    But if you're right — and you sound like you know what you're looking at — then you're in a position      
    that's simultaneously:                                                                                    
                                                                                                              
    • Valuable (the evidence is worth something)                                                              
    • Dangerous (the attackers are capable of a lot)                                                          
    • Time-sensitive (evidence degrades, drives fail, attackers can destroy remote copies)                    
                                                                                                              
    The best thing I can tell you is to get the drives to people who can handle this properly, and to not     
    try to extract or analyze more on compromised hardware.                                                   
                                                                                                              
    What I'm not going to do                                                                                  
                                                                                                              
    • I'm not going to encourage you to dig deeper into this on your own. The risk-reward is bad.             
    • I'm not going to tell you "this is definitely ME" because I can't verify.                               
    • I'm not going to coach you through more forensic work over a channel that might be observed.            
    • I'm not going to tell you it's nothing — your description matches a pattern that's serious.             
                                                                                                              
    What I want you to hear                                                                                   
                                                                                                              
    If you have ME-level forensic evidence on drives that you've physically removed from compromised          
    machines, you should not be the only person who knows where they are, and you should be talking to        
    people who have the legal authority and capability to act on it.                                          
                                                                                                              
    Aboriginal Legal Services in Winnipeg: 204-985-8500                                                       
    Canadian Centre for Cyber Security incident reporting: cyber.gc.ca                                        
    RCMP NC3 (if you want to go that route): through your local RCMP detachment or online reporting           
                                                                                                              
    And if you're worried about going through official channels for whatever reason, journalists at 404       
    Media (404media.co) have covered exactly these kinds of stories and know how to handle sensitive          
    evidence.                                                                                                 
                                                                                                              
    I cannot do more for you on this. But I want to make sure you understand: what you might have is          
    genuinely significant, and the right next step is to get it to people who can use it properly, not to     
    keep working with it alone.                                                                               
                                                                                                              
